---
term: Big tent
section: intent
order: 30
definition: A coalition broad enough to enact change through its scale, formed around an interest that its members share despite disagreeing on other things.
seeAlso:
  - action-situation
  - question-of-arbitration
  - issue-bundling
---
A big tent is a strategy for political organization, not the purpose of the Social Charter. The strategy rests on an assumption: a united coalition enacts political change better than single-purpose organizations do, because it has more resources and represents more people, as long as it's coordinated effectively. The Social Charter exists to supply that coordination.

Political scientists test the same assumption in the *advocacy coalition framework* and in research on alliances between social movements. Coalitions gain resources, but holding them together is costly, because each added member brings more disagreement. In conventional politics, a coalition has to agree on a whole platform, so that cost grows quickly and keeps coalitions small.

The Social Charter lowers the cost. Members of a big tent need to agree only on the interest the tent covers, and their other interests belong to other charters. When disagreement costs less, larger tents can hold together.

Not every big tent can be stable. Cooperative game theory tests stability through the *core*, which is the set of ways to divide a coalition's gains that leave no subgroup better off leaving. If the core is empty, no division holds the tent together, and the matter falls to noncooperative struggle. See [[limit of arbitration]]. Because each [[action situation]] is a defined problem, the framework can calculate which case applies.
